      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "### How to read this honestly\n\nThis dashboard is the reason the project exists, and it is also the easiest one to lie with.\n\n**Most of TaskFlow's p50 is Postgres**, and Postgres is the same process for all five implementations. A `GET /api/v1/tasks` that spends 8ms in the database and 0.3ms in the handler will look nearly identical in Rust and in Python — because you are mostly benchmarking Postgres. A p50 ranking here is close to noise.\n\n**The signal is in the tail and in memory.** p99 shows where a runtime's scheduler, GC pauses or event-loop stalls start costing real requests. RSS shows what each runtime charges you to sit idle, and how that grows under load. Those two are where the languages genuinely differ at this workload.\n\n**Fair-comparison rules** — break any of these and the numbers below are decoration:\n\n* Identical histogram buckets in all five (§5.2, normative). If they differ, `histogram_quantile` is answering a different question per service and the panels are not comparable.\n* Identical container limits — 2 CPUs, 2 GiB, set in `docker-compose.yml`.\n* Same load generator, open-loop, same target rate. Closed-loop tools coordinate-omit and flatter slow services (`scripts/benchmark.sh`).\n* Same cache and rate-limit settings. `CACHE_ENABLED` differing between two runs invalidates both.\n* Warm. Compare after the JIT/AOT tiers have settled, or you are measuring startup.\n\n**The interesting result is not the ranking — it is where the curves cross**, and what makes each one bend. Cold-start time is the fifth comparison metric and is deliberately absent here: it is a one-shot measurement, not a time series. `scripts/benchmark.sh` reports it."
      },
      "transparent": false,
      "pluginVersion": "13.0.3",
      "id": 1,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 13,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      }
    },
